<title>USA: US EXAMINING OPTIONS ON CANADA WHEAT--GLICKMAN.</title>
<headline>US EXAMINING OPTIONS ON CANADA WHEAT--GLICKMAN.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-08-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>The United States is examining options that might be used to curb imports ot Canadian wheat, Agriculture Secretary Dan Glickman said Wednesday.</p>
<p>&quot;You examine what your legal options are in all of this,&quot; Glickman told reporters. &quot;That, I think, is what we're doing now.&quot;</p>
<p>Glickman said the administration was reviewing options available under trade law. He said the administration wanted to avoid setting an adverse precedent that might be harmful later.</p>
<p>&quot;There are some options. Some of them are expensive,&quot; he said.</p>
<p>U.S. imports of Canadian wheat have topped the 1.5 million tonne reference point set by the Clinton administration. Commerce Secretary William Daley met Tuesday with Canadian officials to discuss trade matters.</p>
<p>Wheat-state lawmakers have demanded a cutoff in the shipments.</p>
</text>
